[Event "English vs 1...e5 (Reversed Sicilian)"]

{The English (1.c4) is a Sicilian with an extra tempo. White fights for d5 from the flank and fianchettoes on g2.}
1. c4 {[%csl Gd5] The English: controls d5 from the side. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
1... e5 {The Reversed Sicilian. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. Nc3 {Develops toward d5. The knight on c3 controls d5 and e4.}
2... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
 (2... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls d4 and supports the pawn on e5.}
3. g3 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g3 controls f4 and h4.}
3... g6 {Symmetrical. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
4. Bg2 {[%cal Gg2b7] The bishop rakes the long diagonal. Fianchettoes the bishop on g2, where it rakes the long diagonal through f3, e4 and d5.}
4... Bg7 {Same idea. Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6 and e5.}
5. d3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on d3 controls e4 and supports the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1.}
5... d6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and supports the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
6. e4 {[%csl Gd5] The Botvinnik setup: a clamp on d5. Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
)
3. Nf3 {Develops with pressure on e5. The knight on f3 controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
3... Nc6 {Defends e5. Develops the knight to c6, where it controls d4 and supports the pawn on e5.}
4. g3 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g3 controls f4 and h4.}
4... d5 {Black plays an Open Sicilian a tempo down. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
 (4... Bb4 {Pins the knight. Develops the bishop to b4, where it controls c5 and d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. Bg2 {Long diagonal. Fianchettoes the bishop on g2, where it rakes the long diagonal through f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6... e4 {Gains space, kicking the knight. Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d3 and eyes the knight on f3.}
7. Ne1 {The knight re-routes to c2, and d3 will challenge e4.}
)
5. cxd5 {Takes the pawn on d5; Black will want to recapture.}
5... Nxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.}
6. Bg2 {[%cal Gg2d5] Attacks the knight. Fianchettoes the bishop on g2, where it rakes the long diagonal through f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6... Nb6 {Regroups the knight to b6 and controls d5.}
7. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
7... Be7 {Develops the bishop to e7, where it controls f6 and d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
8. d3 {Solid; prepares Be3 and a queenside expansion with a3 and b4.} *

[Event "English vs 1...c5 & 1...Nf6"]

{The Symmetrical English and the Anglo-Indian lines.}
1. c4 {The English. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
1... c5 {The Symmetrical. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
 (1... Nf6 {The Anglo-Indian. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
2. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
2... e6 {Prepares ...d5. Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
3. e4 {The Mikenas Attack. Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
3... d5 {Counters. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 eyes the pawn on c4 and the pawn on e4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
4. e5 {[%cal Ge5f6] Kicks the knight. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d6 and eyes the knight on f6.}
4... d4 {Black hits the c3-knight. Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls e3 and eyes the knight on c3.}
5. exf6 {Takes the knight. Takes the knight on f6; Black will want to recapture.}
5... dxc3 {Takes back. Takes the knight on c3; White will want to recapture.}
6. bxc3 {Recaptures toward the centre. Recaptures on c3, restoring the material balance.}
6... Qxf6 {Regains the pawn. Takes the pawn on f6, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
7. d4 {[%csl Gd4] A strong centre. The pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
)
2. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and d4.}
2... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
3. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
3... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls d4 and e5.}
4. g3 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g3 controls f4 and h4.}
4... d5 {Breaks in the centre. The pawn on d5 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on c4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
5. cxd5 {Takes the pawn on d5; Black will want to recapture.}
5... Nxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.}
6. Bg2 {[%cal Gg2d5] Hits the knight. Fianchettoes the bishop on g2, where it rakes the long diagonal through f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6... Nc7 {Retreats, keeping d5 covered. The knight goes to c7 and controls d5.}
7. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
7... e5 {The Rubinstein setup: a big centre. The pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.} *
